                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Bullets or details say</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">U20F 15% pad ($115.19)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Max incline</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">15%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">17% (0, 10, 17%)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">U20F 15% pad ($115.19)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Weight</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">—</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">16.5 lb in details, not credible</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">15% pad ($120.99)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Top speed</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">8.7 mph</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0.6–6.2 mph</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">15% pad ($120.99)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Incline steps</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">15%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">"0%, 12% & 15%" and "0%, 10%, and 15%"</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">15% three-screen pad ($151.99)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Incline range</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">15%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">"between 0-16%", then "10% or 15%"</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">18% three-screen pad ($151.98)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Weight</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">—</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">40.8 lb in bullets, 47.8 lb in details</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Unbranded 15% pad ($123.48)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Incline, capacity, motor</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">15%, 350 lb, 3.0 HP</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">16%, 330 lb, 2.5 HP</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">20% auto, 4.0HP ($289.99)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Belt</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">"44.1" Wide"</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">44.1 x 16 in. (44.1 is the length)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">2-20% auto, 3.5HP ($284.99)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Adjustable part</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Handle height</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">"deck height" 45.5–53.5 in.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            The unbranded $123.48 pad is the worst case: its title promises 15% incline, 350 lb and a 3.0 HP motor, and its
            bullets give 16%, 330 lb and 2.5 HP. We would not buy it on its title. The variation listing at $254.99 is a
            different problem: its title lists "3.0/3.5/4.0HP", "330/400LBS" and "6.2/8.0/8.7 MPH" because it bundles
            several versions, and its product details state no warranty. Check which version is selected before you
            pay.
          </p>
          <p>
            One more change is worth knowing about. The steep walker (ASIN B0HH368PZJ) carried a "40% Incline" title when
            we recorded it on 6 November 2026, and our older pages on the <a href="/treadmills-with-the-highest-incline/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills with the highest incline</a> described it that way. On 27 November the same listing said 32%, with five steps of 0, 8, 16, 24 and 32%, and
            the deck still 15 inches high at the top. Listings change; the figure that counts is the one on the page
            when you order.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="black-friday" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>CURSOR FITNESS Prices on Black Friday 2026</h2>
<p>
            Every price on this page is the price Amazon showed on 27 November 2026, Black Friday. Eleven of the fourteen
            CURSOR FITNESS listings carried Amazon's own "Black Friday Deal" label; the under-desk pad, the 32% steep walker
            and one Woot-sold unbranded pad did not. For the labelled listings, the deal window shown ran from 19 November
            to the end of 30 November, Pacific time, so the same prices should hold through <a href="/cyber-monday-treadmill-deals/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Cyber Monday</a> unless stock runs
            out. None of the CURSOR FITNESS prices was a Prime-exclusive offer: Amazon marked every deal as open to all
            buyers.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Cheapest overall:</strong> $94.99 for the unbranded 15% pad sold by RUNHOME, against a $144.99 list
            price. A second offer on the same listing was $119.99.</li>
            <li><strong>Cheapest CURSOR FITNESS-titled pad:</strong> the U20F at $115.19 against $199.99, the largest
            cut in percentage terms (42%) alongside the 4.0 HP treadmill.</li>
            <li><strong>Biggest cut in dollars:</strong> the 4.0 HP auto-incline treadmill, $289.99 against a $499.99
            list price.</li>
            <li><strong>Smallest cut:</strong> the under-desk pad, $119.99 against $129.99, with no deal label.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            A cut against a list price is not the same as a good price. Several of these listings show a list price
            that is far above anything a comparable pad sells for, and the 4.0 HP treadmill's $499.99 list price is $210
            above its sale price. Compare the price on the day with similar pads rather than the percentage off. Our <a href="/black-friday-walking-pad-deals/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Black Friday walking pad deals</a> page does that comparison across brands, and four listings showed a second, higher offer from the same seller,
            so check that the price in your basket is the one you saw.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>CURSOR FITNESS Warranty, Returns and Who Sells It</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Every CURSOR FITNESS listing that states a warranty gives one year. We found no brand-run warranty
            policy, returns page or support contact outside Amazon, so on an Amazon order the listing, the seller and
            Amazon's own process are what you rely on.</strong>
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What the listings say</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Warranty length:</strong> the product details give "1 Year", "1 YEAR", "1YEARS", "1 Year
            Manufacturer" or "1 Year Warranty" on thirteen listings. The $254.99 variation listing gives none.</li>
            <li><strong>What it covers:</strong> only the under-desk pad's bullets spell it out: "a manufacturer's
            1-year coverage for defects", with support "reachable through Amazon messaging". No listing mentions
            labour, parts-only terms or a longer frame or motor warranty.</li>
            <li><strong>Support route:</strong> two listings tell you to go to Your Orders, find the order and click
            "Contact Seller"; several promise "24/7" support. That is RUNHOME's or Woot's support, through Amazon.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Who sells it, and why it matters</h3>
          <p>
            RUNHOME sold ten of the fourteen listings and Woot the other four (three of them the unbranded pads). Neither is
            Amazon.com. With a third-party seller, the return window, who pays return shipping on a 30 to 60 lb box and how
            a warranty claim is handled are set by that seller within Amazon's rules, so read the "Returns" line beside the
            price before you order. Woot is owned by Amazon but runs its own returns terms, so check those too.
          </p>
          <p>
            In practice, keep the box until you are sure the pad works, photograph the serial label, and record a short
            video of any fault before you contact the seller. That applies to any budget walking pad, but it matters more
            when there is no brand website to fall back on. If you would rather buy from Amazon itself, the UREVO Strol 2E
            Pro in the cards was sold by Amazon.com on 27 November 2026.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-to-check" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What to Check Before You Buy a CURSOR FITNESS Walking Pad</h2>
<ul>
            <li><strong>The incline in the bullets, not the title.</strong> Three listings give one maximum in the title
            and another in the bullets. Assume the lower figure, and do not pay more for a bigger number.</li>
            <li><strong>The seller and the second offer.</strong> Check whether RUNHOME or Woot is selling, and whether
            the price you saw is the featured offer. Four listings had a second offer from the same seller, $25 to $48 higher.</li>
            <li><strong>The belt length.</strong> 35.8 to 38.6 inches on the pads and 42.5 to 44.1 on the treadmills. If you
            are over about 6 ft or have a long stride, the longer belts are the safer choice even for walking.</li>
            <li><strong>Manual or automatic incline.</strong> Every CURSOR FITNESS pad whose listing explains its incline
            uses a manual one, set by hand with the belt stopped. Only the three treadmills change incline under power.</li>
            <li><strong>The weight limit on the version you pick.</strong> The variation listing offers 330 and 400 lb
            versions under one title. Make sure the selected version is the one you need.</li>
            <li><strong>Where it will live.</strong> The pads fold to about 4 inches high; the 32% walker folds to 6.1
            inches but is 57.3 inches long; the 3.5 HP treadmill folds to 6.5 inches high. Measure the space under the bed
            or sofa first.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If you are choosing between a pad and a treadmill in the first place, the trade-offs on belt, speed and
            storage are set out in our <a href="/walking-pad-buying-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad buying guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-it-suits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Who a CURSOR FITNESS Treadmill Suits, and Who It Does Not</h2>
<h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">A good fit</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Desk walkers on a budget.</strong> The under-desk pad at $119.99 is flat, quiet by its listing's
            account, arrives assembled and has a handle, which is all most people need for slow walking at a standing
            desk.</li>
            <li><strong>Incline walkers who want something to hold.</strong> Most CURSOR FITNESS pads have side handles and
            a manual incline, the combination people look for when they want a harder walk without a full treadmill.</li>
            <li><strong>Heavier walkers on a tight budget.</strong> The three-screen pads and treadmills state 400 lb, more
            than many pads at this price.</li>
            <li><strong>People who want to try steep walking cheaply.</strong> The X5FD reaches 32% for $199.99. Our <a href="/best-treadmills-for-hiking/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills for hiking</a> guide
            compares it with powered machines that cost far more.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">A poor fit</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Runners.</strong> No CURSOR FITNESS belt is longer than 44.1 inches, and the fastest model tops out
            at 8.7 mph. If you plan to run, budget for a treadmill with a longer belt and a published continuous motor
            rating.</li>
            <li><strong>Anyone who wants to change incline mid-walk on a pad.</strong> The pads' incline is manual; only
            the treadmills offer powered incline.</li>
            <li><strong>Buyers who want a brand to deal with directly.</strong> There is no brand store, phone line or
            published warranty policy we could find; support runs through the Amazon seller.</li>
            <li><strong>Anyone needing exact figures.</strong> If a precise incline, weight or top speed matters, choose a
            listing whose figures agree, such as the under-desk pad, or a better-documented brand.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>CURSOR FITNESS Alternatives: Better-Known Brands and Powered Incline</h2>
<p>
            The alternative in the cards covers the most common reason CURSOR FITNESS shoppers look elsewhere: wanting to
            buy from Amazon itself and a brand with a longer US record. The UREVO Strol 2E Pro cost $177.64 on 27 November
            2026, sold by Amazon.com, with a safety handle, a 12% manual incline, a running mode to 6.2 mph and a 12-month
            warranty. Its listing does not state a capacity or belt size, so check both.
          </p>
          <p>
            If manual incline is the sticking point and you do not need handles, the MERACH W50 Lite <a href="/how-much-does-a-walking-pad-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>walking pad cost</a> $199.99 the same day, sold by MERACHFITNESS and labelled a Black Friday Deal, with a 10-level automatic incline
            to 10% changed by remote, a 350 lb rating and a one-year warranty. Its bullets give no speed range or belt
            size. Our roundup of the <a href="/best-walking-pads/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best walking pads</a> compares both with other pads that publish fuller figures.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Other budget brands read the same way</h3>
          <p>
            CURSOR FITNESS sits in a crowded part of Amazon. Our guide to <a href="/ypoo-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">YPOO treadmills</a> covers a similar range of
            incline pads with handles, from a brand that does run its own US store and publishes its warranty terms. Our
            guide to <a href="/serenelife-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">SereneLife treadmills</a> covers
            another budget label with conflicting listing figures. Both are worth a look if you want to compare like for
            like before Cyber Monday on 30 November.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Buying a CURSOR FITNESS Walking Pad? Avoid These 4 Mistakes</h2>
          <p>A CURSOR FITNESS pad can be a sensible cheap incline walker. These are the mistakes that turn it into a return.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Believing the incline in the title</h3>
              <p>Three CURSOR FITNESS listings give one maximum incline in the title and another in the bullets, and one steep walker changed from 40% to 32% within a month. Read the bullets on the day and assume the lower figure.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Buying an unbranded listing on its title</h3>
              <p>Four listings under the CURSOR FITNESS brand do not name it in the title, and one of them gives 15%, 350 lb and 3.0 HP in the title but 16%, 330 lb and 2.5 HP in the bullets. Check the brand line and the bullets before you compare prices.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Not checking the version on a variation listing</h3>
              <p>The $254.99 auto-incline listing bundles 3.0, 3.5 and 4.0 HP versions rated 330 or 400 lb, and states no warranty. Confirm the selected version before you pay.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Buying one to run on</h3>
              <p>Every CURSOR FITNESS pad belt is 35.8 to 38.6 inches long, and the treadmills stop at 44.1 inches and 8.7 mph. If you plan to run, budget for a treadmill with a longer belt and a higher top speed.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>Our verdict on the brand</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">CURSOR FITNESS is a real Amazon budget brand that makes incline walking pads, not
          running treadmills.</strong> The U20F 15% pad at $115.19 is the one to buy on price, the under-desk pad at
          $119.99 is the most consistent listing, the 18% pad at $151.98 adds a 400 lb rating, the X5FD reaches 32% for
          $199.99, and the 2-20% auto-incline treadmill at $284.99 is the only one for a light jog (Amazon, 27 Nov 2026).
          Every model has a short belt, most are sold by RUNHOME, and the warranty is one year.
          </p>
          <p>
            Read the bullets rather than the title, assume the lower incline and capacity where they disagree, check the
          seller and the version, and keep the box. If you want to buy from Amazon itself, or need a longer belt, compare <a href="/best-walking-pads/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">the best walking pads</a> or our <a href="/best-walking-pads-with-incline/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">incline walking pads</a> before you order.
          </p>
        </div>

